id: pi
name: Pi

Register Birks AI as a custom OpenAI-compatible provider in Pi's model configuration.

--- file ~/.pi/agent/models.json (json) ---
{
  "providers": {
    "birks-ai": {
      "baseUrl": "https://ai.birks.dev/v1",
      "api": "openai-completions",
      "apiKey": "BIRKS_AI_API_KEY",
      "authHeader": true,
      "models": [
        { "id": "Qwen3-Coder-30B" }
      ]
    }
  }
}

--- commands (bash) ---
export BIRKS_AI_API_KEY="YOUR_API_KEY"

pi

--- notes ---
- The apiKey field names an environment variable, not the credential itself, so BIRKS_AI_API_KEY stays out of the file.
- authHeader: true makes Pi send Authorization: Bearer, which is what the gateway expects.
- If you already have a models.json, merge the providers entry rather than replacing the file.

--- agent prompt ---
Configure Pi on this machine to use my self-hosted Birks AI endpoint at https://ai.birks.dev. Add a custom provider to ~/.pi/agent/models.json using the openai-completions API with authHeader enabled, merging with any existing providers. Use the model "Qwen3-Coder-30B". Read the API key from the BIRKS_AI_API_KEY environment variable. Do not write the key itself into any file in a git repository, and do not print it. Preserve my existing unrelated configuration.
